response:
 Start by expressing the cost of a vanilla bar in terms of $c$ \begin{align} \text{vanilla bar}&=c+2 \end{align} 
Now find the amount spent by Jamie and Kevin.
$Jamie$ spoke <think> First, find the amount Jamie spends when he buys 1 chocolate bar \begin{align} 1\times c=\boxed {c} ~\text{dollars} \end{align} Jamie then sells three vanilla bars \begin{align} 1\times (c+2) = c+2 \end{align} Jamie's total cost for the three vanilla bars is \begin{align} 3(c+2)=3c+6 \end{align} So Jamie's total cost is \begin{align} c+(3c+6)=(4c+6) \end{align} 
Next, let's find how much Kevin spends. \begin{align} 5c= \boxed {5c} ~\text{dollars} \end{align} Kevin will pay less than Jamie because he buys five chocolate bars instead of four. The difference between the amount of money Jamie and Kevin spent is$5c-(4c+6) = c-6$.
So Jamie and Kevin spent a total of $(5c)+(4c+6)+(c-6) = 10c$ dollars at the candy store. </think> $\fbox{ 10 c }$

